## TIME ZONES — NEVER READ A DAY OR HOUR IN UTC

A stream time only means something to a creator as their own wall-clock time.
This rule exists because the mistake has been made repeatedly, including
deliberately: `app/dashboard/page.js` still carries comments reading
*"FIX: use getUTCHours() — tips stored in UTC"*.

**That reasoning is wrong.** Timestamps being *stored* in UTC is true and
irrelevant. Storage format and display basis are different questions. A tip
stored as `2026-09-15T05:00:00Z` was earned at 10 PM on Monday by a creator in
Los Angeles. Reading it in UTC calls it 5 AM Tuesday — a different hour and a
different day.

What this broke in practice:

- Stream Planner bucketed by `getUTCDay()`/`getUTCHours()`, then converted for
  display using a sample date in January — so every hour displayed one hour
  early in any daylight-saving timezone, and the weekday was wrong for anyone
  west of UTC. The feature whose whole job is saying when to stream was wrong
  for every creator.
- `missedRevenueByPlatform` compared a UTC session hour against a local
  `bestHour`, so the dollar figure it reported was computed from a mismatch.
- Money Map bucketed hours locally but keyed dates in UTC, putting a
  late-evening tip on the following day.

### The rule

Creator-facing days and hours are read on the creator's clock:

```js
import { toLocalHour, toLocalDow } from '@/lib/analytics'
const hour = toLocalHour(tip.date_time, userTz)
const dow  = toLocalDow(tip.date_time, userTz)
```

In a client component the browser clock is already the creator's, so plain
`.getHours()` / `.getDay()` is correct there — but never mix the two bases in
one feature.

`getUTCFullYear/Month/Date` are acceptable for duration arithmetic. They are
not acceptable as a **date key** sitting beside a locally-bucketed hour: that
splits one feature across two clocks.

### Enforcement

`scripts/check-timezone.mjs` runs in CI and fails the build on any new
`getUTCDay()`/`getUTCHours()`. It carries a baseline of pre-existing sites so
old debt does not block every PR. **Lower a baseline number when you fix one;
never raise one.** If a flagged use really is duration arithmetic, add it to
the baseline with a comment explaining why.

Regression coverage: `lib/analytics/__tests__/timezone-consistency.test.js`.

